<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
Release-scoped upgrade data for the NEXT Darpan release (unreleased).
Assembled from the generic seed files under runtime/component/darpan/data/ since v1.5.1, then
hand-ordered. Load with: ./gradlew loadDarpanUpgradeData

REBUILT, NOT APPENDED. This file previously held 1.5.1's pack; that pack is frozen at
data/releases/1.5.1/upgrade-data.xml and must not be edited. This is a DELTA carrying only what
changed after 1.5.1. An environment that has not yet loaded 1.5.1 must load
data/releases/1.5.1/upgrade-data.xml FIRST — this file restates a row that pack also restates, and
ordering the two the other way leaves the 1.5.1 values in place.

ORDERING: SourceSystemConnector before SourceSystemConnectorField. Moqui's entity-facade loader
inserts rows in DOCUMENT ORDER — it does not topologically sort — so a child ahead of its parent
fails with a raw *_ibfk_* constraint error that points nowhere near this file. release_preflight.py
sorts alphabetically and WILL break that chain if this file is regenerated. Re-order by hand after.

OPERATOR IMPACT: 2 records.

  - SourceSystemConnector SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS is restated (create-or-update by primary key, so it
    changes only the named columns) with keepFieldsBase swapping the withdrawn returnWorkflowStatus
    for orderReturnStatus. filterParameterName is unchanged from 1.5.1 and is restated only because
    create-or-update writes whole rows.

  - SourceSystemConnectorField $.records[*].orderReturnStatus is new: the "Order return status" pill,
    and after this load the endpoint's ONLY return-status pill.

WHAT THIS CHANGES FOR OPERATORS, and why it is worth the churn so soon after 1.5.1. 1.5.1 shipped a
pill whose values no operator could discover. It held Shopify's Return.status, whose in-progress
value is spelled OPEN — a word that appears on no surface an operator can reach, because the rules
board renders a pill's label and field path but never its description, and SourceSystemConnectorField
has no allowed-values column at all. Meanwhile Shopify's own admin shows "Return in progress" and its
order search spells it return_status:in_progress. So the natural rule, exclude IN_PROGRESS, matched
nothing and reported a zero count — which reads exactly like a feature that was never deployed.
This pack replaces it with Order.returnStatus, whose values ARE the ones Shopify shows operators:
IN_PROGRESS, INSPECTION_COMPLETE, NO_RETURN, RETURN_FAILED, RETURN_REQUESTED, RETURNED.

Being an order-wide aggregate, it also lands on REFUND rows, which the withdrawn per-return field
could never reach. Excluding IN_PROGRESS therefore drops an in-progress order's refund rows as well
as its return. That is deliberate, and it is a real behaviour change: if OMS holds such a refund, it
will now report as missing-in-Shopify.

Both records need the matching shopify-darpan build deployed alongside — the component that emits
orderReturnStatus. Against an older build the pill appears but selects a field the extract does not
contain, so every rule on it matches nothing, silently, with no error anywhere.

ONE MANUAL STEP, and skipping it leaves a pill that can never match. A seed load is createOrUpdate and
never deletes, so this file INSERTS the new pill but leaves the withdrawn
$.records[*].returnWorkflowStatus behind (and, on an environment that loaded the few-hour-old original,
$.records[*].returnStatus as well). The board would offer a status pill that no longer exists on the
extract. Both PKs are declared in SourceSystemConnectorFieldWriteSupport.RETIRED_FIELDS, which the
RETIRED_FIELDS migration deletes — but that migration is ALREADY APPLIED on every environment that ran
1.5.0's migrations, and run#PendingMigrations skips anything already applied. It must therefore be
re-run explicitly ONCE per environment, after this file loads:

    admin.MigrationAdminServices.run#Migration  migrationId=RETIRED_FIELDS  force=true

(the Migrations screen's per-migration run with force set does the same). It is idempotent — a second
run deletes nothing. Running it on an environment that never held the stale rows is a safe no-op and
is the recommended default, because telling the cases apart from outside the database is not worth the
operator's time.

Any exclusion rule already saved against returnWorkflowStatus keeps its stored expression and will
match nothing after this load. Re-add it on the new pill, with value IN_PROGRESS — note the value
changes too, not just the field: REQUESTED,OPEN becomes IN_PROGRESS.
-->
<entity-facade-xml type="darpan-seed">

    <!-- Shopify per-EVENT (refund or return) records for returns reconciliation. Restated from 1.5.1
         to swap the withdrawn returnWorkflowStatus out of keepFieldsBase for orderReturnStatus. Every
         other column below is unchanged and is restated only because create-or-update writes whole
         rows. -->
    <darpan.reconciliation.SourceSystemConnector
            systemEnumId="SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS"
            extractServiceName="reconciliation.ShopifyOrderExtractionServices.extract#ShopifyOrderReturnRefs"
            dateFromParameterName="windowStart"
            dateToParameterName="windowEnd"
            expectedSourceConfigType="SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS_API"
            configParameterName="shopifyAuthConfigId"
            configEntityName="darpan.shopify.ShopifyAuthConfig"
            remoteId="SHOPIFY_REMOTE"
            endpointLabel="Shopify Return References"
            systemAliases="SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS,SHOPIFY_RETURNS,DAR_SYS_SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS"
            preserveWindowInstants="N"
            filterParameterName="sourceFilters"
            keepFieldsBase="refundOrReturnId,refundOrReturnType,orderId,createdAt,orderCancelledAt,refundLineEverFulfilled,orderReturnStatus"
            lookupServiceName="reconciliation.ShopifyOrderExtractionServices.lookup#ShopifyRefundOrReturnIds"
            lookupIdsParameterName="refundOrReturnIds"
            lookupMaxIds="10000"
            enabled="Y"/>

    <!-- The rules-board pill the connector change exists for. FKs the SourceSystemConnector
         immediately above by systemEnumId. -->
    <darpan.reconciliation.SourceSystemConnectorField systemEnumId="SHOPIFY_RETURN_REFS" fieldPath="$.records[*].orderReturnStatus"
            label="Order return status" fieldType="string" sequenceNum="5"
            description="ADDED 2026-09-01 (DAR-BE-026), replacing the withdrawn returnWorkflowStatus pill —
                see the retirement entry in SourceSystemConnectorFieldWriteSupport.RETIRED_FIELDS. This is
                the endpoint's ONLY return-status field. Holds Shopify's own Order.returnStatus, an
                OrderReturnStatus: IN_PROGRESS, INSPECTION_COMPLETE, NO_RETURN, RETURN_FAILED,
                RETURN_REQUESTED, RETURNED. Those are the values to type, and they are the ones Shopify
                itself shows: the admin renders IN_PROGRESS as 'Return in progress' and the order search
                spells it return_status:in_progress. The withdrawn field held a per-return ReturnStatus
                instead, whose in-progress value is spelled OPEN — a word on no surface an operator could
                see — so excluding IN_PROGRESS matched nothing and reported a zero count, which reads
                exactly like an undeployed feature. Present on EVERY row, refund and return alike, because
                it describes the whole ORDER: excluding IN_PROGRESS therefore drops an in-progress order's
                refund rows as well as its return, which is deliberate — the per-return field it replaced
                could never reach a refund row at all. Key always present, null when Shopify reports
                nothing, so a consumer can tell 'extract predates the field' from 'Shopify said nothing'.
                Exclude IN_PROGRESS to drop orders whose return has not finished yet."/>

</entity-facade-xml>
